invariant: INV-26
file: ghash_vector.h
catches: ghash_equiv_test
reason: the host object's GHASH pass multiplies its later blocks by the powers of
  H in the wrong order, the third block by H^3 where a pass of eight blocks
  needs H^6, so every whole pass hashes to the wrong value. It is the
  mistake an index that counts the other way invites. CBMC cannot read the
  instruction, so bin/ghash_equiv_test is what holds it: it runs every
  data length through three passes of eight blocks against gcm.c's
  portable loop, one block at a time. The edit is shared C after either
  instruction, so it lands on an ARMv8 runner and an x86-64 one alike.

--- old
        ghash_add_products(s, &s->sums, ghash_load_block(&data[j * AES_BLOCK]),
                           GHASH_PASS_BLOCKS - 1 - j);
--- new
        ghash_add_products(s, &s->sums, ghash_load_block(&data[j * AES_BLOCK]), j);
